\subsection{\code{method_gamlss}}{

Estimates the conditional outcome mean and the conditional density of exposure with generalized additive models for location, scale, and shape (GAMLSS) using functions in the \CRANpkg{gamlss} package. GAMLSS are related to generalized linear models but include models for the whole conditional density of the response variable, not just the mean. They can also include additive smoothing terms for continuous covariates. First, a model for the exposure given the covariates is fit to arrive at estimates for the parameters of the conditional exposure distribution. These are used directly to compute the conditional exposure density. Second, a model for the outcome given the exposure and covariates is fit to arrive at estimates for the parameters of the conditional outcome distribution, from which the conditional outcome mean is estimated.

For each model, a distribution must be chosen. Ideally, this should be one that matches the nature of the response variables, e.g., a model with natural number support when the response is a count. See \pkgfun{gamlss.dist}{gamlss.family} for options; a family can be supplied as a family object (e.g., \code{gamlss.dist::NO()}), the function that creates one (\code{gamlss.dist::NO}), or its name (\code{"NO"}). Families from \pkg{stats}, such as \code{binomial()}, are not accepted. For the outcome, the default is \pkgfun{gamlss.dist}{BI}, the binomial distribution with a logit link, when the outcome takes two values, and \pkgfun{gamlss.dist}{SST}, the skew-t type 3 distribution with 4 parameters and support for the real numbers, otherwise. A binary outcome must be coded as 0 and 1, and its outcome model is then a logistic regression. \code{SST} is parameterized by its mean and standard deviation and restricts its degrees of freedom to be greater than 2, so the conditional outcome mean always exists; \pkgfun{gamlss.dist}{ST3}, the same distribution in its original parameterization, has no mean where its fitted degrees of freedom are 1 or less, and an error is thrown when the outcome model yields such a fit. For the exposure, the default is \pkgfun{gamlss.dist}{ST3}, for which only the density is needed.

